---
/**
 * /debug/rendering — the deterministic rendering surface (WINTAGE_WEB_CONTRACT
 * §25, §26).
 *
 * Its job is to make the things a visual acceptance pass has to judge
 * inspectable: the CSS viewport, the device pixel ratio, which palette is
 * actually active, and integer-pixel samples of the laws this site claims. The
 * static content renders without JavaScript and says what it cannot know; the
 * live numbers fill in progressively.
 *
 * Two limits are stated on the page rather than papered over: this route cannot
 * detect physical browser zoom, and it cannot prove that the compositor is
 * rasterizing glyphs without antialiasing. Both are reported as hints, never as
 * facts, and nothing here blocks on them.
 */
import MaturityBadge from '../../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { ROUTES } from '../../data/site';
import { DEFAULT_THEME_SLUG } from '../../themes/schema';
import { defaultTheme, themes } from '../../themes';
import { THEME_CHANGE_EVENT, themeDiagnosticsScript } from '../../themes/runtime';

const route = ROUTES.find((entry) => entry.href === '/debug/rendering/');

/** Fills the [data-render-diag] fields. Page-local: it is not theme state. */
const renderDiagnosticsScript = `(function(){
var W='[data-render-diag]';
function fill(){
  var de=document.documentElement;
  var cs=getComputedStyle(de);
  var V={
    viewport: window.innerWidth+' x '+window.innerHeight+' px (CSS pixels)',
    dpr: String(window.devicePixelRatio),
    screen: window.screen.width+' x '+window.screen.height+' px',
    'font-ui': cs.fontFamily,
    smoothing: (cs.webkitFontSmoothing||'not reported')+' / text-rendering: '+cs.textRendering,
    canvas: cs.backgroundColor+' on html, '+getComputedStyle(document.body).backgroundColor+' on body',
    theme: de.getAttribute('data-theme')||'(none)'
  };
  var nodes=document.querySelectorAll(W);
  for(var i=0;i<nodes.length;i++){
    var f=nodes[i].getAttribute('data-render-diag');
    if(V[f]!==undefined)nodes[i].textContent=V[f];
  }
}
fill();
window.addEventListener('resize',fill);
document.addEventListener('${THEME_CHANGE_EVENT}',fill);
})();`;

const PENDING = 'populated by JavaScript on load';
---

<SiteLayout title="SAIPEN Protocol — Rendering diagnostics" tag="/debug/rendering/">
  <p><MaturityBadge level={route?.maturity ?? 'draft'} note="deterministic rendering surface" /></p>
  <h1>Rendering diagnostics</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    The measurements below are the ones a Wintage visual acceptance pass needs and cannot get from
    a screenshot alone: the CSS viewport, the device pixel ratio, which palette the document is
    actually carrying, and the font stack the browser resolved. Fields that need JavaScript say so
    until it runs; nothing on this page is hidden or broken without it.
  </p>

  <h2>Live measurements</h2>
  <dl class="diag">
    <dt>CSS viewport</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="viewport">{PENDING}</dd>

    <dt>devicePixelRatio</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="dpr">{PENDING}</dd>

    <dt>screen (CSS px)</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="screen">{PENDING}</dd>

    <dt>active theme slug</dt>
    <dd data-theme-diag="slug">{defaultTheme.slug}</dd>

    <dt>active theme label</dt>
    <dd data-theme-diag="label">{defaultTheme.label}</dd>

    <dt>persisted theme</dt>
    <dd data-theme-diag="persisted">(needs JavaScript)</dd>

    <dt>canonical default</dt>
    <dd data-theme-diag="default">{DEFAULT_THEME_SLUG}</dd>

    <dt>themes registered</dt>
    <dd data-theme-diag="count">{themes.length}</dd>

    <dt>resolved font stack (UI)</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="font-ui">{PENDING}</dd>

    <dt>smoothing hint</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="smoothing">{PENDING}</dd>

    <dt>canvas colour</dt>
    <dd data-render-diag="canvas">{PENDING}</dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="w-panel__note">
    The theme fields above follow the site-wide selector: change the palette at the top of this page
    and they update, because they listen for the same <code class="w-code-inline">sai-theme-change</code>
    notification the selector emits.
  </p>

  <h2>Zoom and scale — what is and is not claimed</h2>
  <div class="w-panel w-panel--sunken">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
      <strong>Reference condition: 100% browser zoom.</strong> Screenshots taken for review are
      captured at 100% zoom in a Chromium-family browser at the reference viewport, so the pixel
      samples below mean what they say.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      This page does <strong>not</strong> detect physical browser zoom. <code class="w-code-inline">devicePixelRatio</code>
      is the only signal available to script, and it cannot separate OS display scaling, browser zoom
      and a high-DPI panel from each other — so the value is reported raw and never interpreted as a
      zoom percentage. There is no blocking or layout behaviour based on DPR or zoom: the samples
      below are integer-aligned by construction, not corrected at runtime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Integer grid and bevel samples</h2>
  <div class="row">
    <div>
      <div class="px-grid" />
      <p class="bench-caption">1px rules on an 8px pitch</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="px-raster" />
      <p class="bench-caption">2&#215;2 raster scaled 32&#215;, <code class="w-code-inline">image-rendering: pixelated</code></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="w-raised" style="padding:var(--gap-5);background-color:var(--ui-raised);width:140px">
        <p style="margin:0">raised — 2px</p>
        <p class="w-panel__note">top/left light, bottom/right dark</p>
      </div>
      <p class="bench-caption">raised bevel</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="w-sunken" style="padding:var(--gap-5);background-color:var(--ui-window);width:140px">
        <p style="margin:0">sunken — 2px</p>
        <p class="w-panel__note">top/left dark, bottom/right light</p>
      </div>
      <p class="bench-caption">sunken bevel</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="w-panel__note">
    Both bevels are pure 2px borders with zero radius, so they land on device pixels at DPR 1 and stay
    hard edges at fractional DPR instead of blurring like a shadow would. The raster sample uses
    nearest-neighbour scaling and no transform: a smooth checkerboard here means something upstream
    is resampling a pixel asset, which is a defect.
  </p>

  <h2>Font rendering sample</h2>
  <div class="font-sample">
    <p class="t-title t-bold">16px title — SAIPEN Protocol</p>
    <p class="t-heading t-bold">14px heading — verification contract</p>
    <p class="t-body">12px body — the default UI size. Ambiguous glyphs: Il1 O0 rg mw.</p>
    <p class="t-compact">11px compact — O0 Il1 rg mw</p>
    <p class="t-meta">10px metadata — O0 Il1 rg mw</p>
  </div>
  <p class="w-panel__note">
    Each size uses its own pixel face (<code class="w-code-inline">SAI Pixel 10-16</code>, built from DejaVu Sans),
    plus the <code class="w-code-inline">-webkit-font-smoothing: none</code> and
    <code class="w-code-inline">text-rendering: optimizeSpeed</code> hints. The hints are not the proof: the
    faces' whole-pixel outlines are, and <code class="w-code-inline">npm run test:pixel</code> checks the rendered
    pixels of every page. Browser zoom and fractional OS scaling still resample text; this route reports what the
    browser resolved and does not measure physical zoom.
  </p>

  <h2>Sample routes</h2>
  <p class="bench-caption">
    This route is registered in <code class="w-code-inline">src/data/site.ts</code> with maturity
    <strong>{route?.maturity ?? 'draft'}</strong> and is deliberately absent from the main menu.
  </p>
  <div class="row">
    <a class="w-button" href="/debug/themes/">Theme bench</a>
    <a class="w-button" href="/debug/components/">Component bench</a>
    <a class="w-button" href="/">Home</a>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>

<script is:inline set:html={themeDiagnosticsScript()} />
<script is:inline set:html={renderDiagnosticsScript} />
